# Section 5: the orientation template — coverage by the canonical guards (T-cover)

**Generated by a program that is not included in this repository** (from a fixed text; the data and
the certificates are in `OrientCoverData.lean`).

`docs/section5/08_proof_plan.md` §7 (T-cover), §9.  For an arbitrary simple counter-clockwise
polygon `P` containing the template chain (`ChainAt P addORV a`) whose other edges avoid `addORR`:

* the **template region** `addORTR` (a finite union of convex integer pieces) contains the filled
  region of the closed template chain `addORK` (the chain closed by the **mouth** `addORMouth`, the
  segment from the last chain vertex `e_j` back to the first one), `addORK_filled_subset`; `addORK.filled`
  is a room of `P` with the mouth as door (`addOR_room`) and `addORTR ⊆ P.filled` (`addORTR_subset`);
* **coverage** (`addOR_cover`): for guard values in `[1/2, 2]` satisfying the constraint `u_A + u_B ≥ 5/2`
  (the guard of `r'_i` having the value `u` of `r_i`, exact weak copy), every point of `addORTR` is
  seen by one of the canonical guards: `addORA u` (`r'_i`), `addORB v` (`r_j`), the stationary guard `p = addORC`, `addORI u`
  (`r_i`) and the stationary guard `g`.  The zone `Γ` is covered by the mechanism lemma
  `addOR_cover_Zg2` (the argument of `addOR_gamma_iff` on a possibly enlarged zone), the nook by the two-dimensional nook zone `addORCvZn2` (`CopyPair.nook_covered`);
* **the mouth** (`addOR_mouth_sees`, `addOR_sees_beyond_mouth`): the guard of `r_j` sees the mouth,
  hence every point beyond it in the angle of the mouth that is seen from the whole mouth.
-/

